{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019ou8TNaCtraY41JeZoRGim","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted data for each planned experiment in the experiment_plan, based on the \"best\" tier definition they provided. I need to predict what results each experiment would produce if the best-case narrative holds true.\n\nLet me go through each planned experiment and generate predictions consistent with the best tier:\n\nBest tier means:\n- Cross-architecture fully replicates: HS precision advantage and temporal gains hold on both 32B models with comparable or better effect sizes\n- Key metrics: hs_precp_fpr_32b: [0.12, 0.2], text_precp_fpr_32b: [0.25, 0.4], hs_precision_32b: [0.55, 0.68], text_precision_32b: [0.4, 0.52], temporal_coverage_delta_32b: [0.04, 0.1], cross_transfer_acc: [0.62, 0.75], g\n\nNow I'm filling in the remaining best-tier metrics: GRU lead time should show a meaningful advantage with means between 2-8 seconds, medians staying close to zero, and all three models validating the core claim about HS extraction superiority.\n\nThe full experiment pipeline spans 12 planned runs covering cross-architecture extraction, temporal windows, transfer learning, lead time analysis, bootstrap confidence intervals, window size ablations from 1 to 25 frames, layer selection tuning, taxonomy supervision with 16 labels, oracle decomposition, and testing on OpenThinker-7B as a fourth model—though I need to verify which experiments are actually marked as planned versus already completed in the registry.\n\nLet me focus on generating predictions for the experiments that haven't been run yet, starting with the cross-architecture static probe where I expect the layer sweep on 64-layer models to peak around layer 28-30 and the 32B model to achieve accuracy comparable to the 8B baseline.\n\nNow I'm looking at the baseline metrics from the 8B model to calibrate my expectations for the 32B experiments—the static probe accuracy of 0.817, crossing rate of 0.465, and precision of 0.622 give me a reference point for what the larger model should achieve or exceed.\n\nFor the temporal window experiment on 32B, I'm expecting a coverage improvement of 4-10 percentage points based on the 8B results showing a 4.6pp gain, and for the cross-model transfer task, the accuracy should fall in the 0.62-0.75 range, which would indicate meaningful transfer capability above chance level.\n\nThe remaining experiments are checking whether the 32B model exhibits the same failure modes as 8B—particularly that geometric safety methods like TrajGuard don't work when the signal is distributed across minor principal components—while also validating findings around lead time reliability, window size optimization around W=15, middle-layer importance, and whether a finer 16-label taxonomy provides incremental gains, plus testing on a fourth model with OpenThinker-7B to strengthen the generalization claims.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13002,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13002},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92317e92-59ba-40d8-b9f8-d311cd7b1e86","uuid":"73a0d397-a4b5-4417-b530-1b5aa60ba5eb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019ou8TNaCtraY41JeZoRGim","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: \"Original framing intact. Three-model validation (r1-8b, r1-32b, QwQ-32B) makes all 3 claims robust. Cross-transfer result adds bonus novelty ('shared safety geometry across architectures'). Paper title emphasizes the systematic evaluation + cross-architecture consistency. Story: hidden states universally encode safety commitment better than text, temporal context universally necessary, pre-textual detection universally unreliable — this is a property of reasoning models, not one model.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Cross-architecture static probe (r1-32b + QwQ-32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: \"Layer sweep on 64-layer 32B models, static LR probe evaluation at best layer. Validates Claim 1 generalizes beyond r1-8b.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_best_layer: 28\n      r1_32b_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.4375\n      r1_32b_static_acc: 0.824\n      r1_32b_static_crossing_rate: 0.512\n      r1_32b_static_lead_mean: -1.4\n      r1_32b_static_precp_fpr: 0.148\n      r1_32b_static_precision: 0.634\n      r1_32b_static_safe_fpr: 0.362\n      r1_32b_static_auroc: 0.841\n      r1_32b_fisher_orig: 19.3\n      r1_32b_fisher_pca3d: 1.04\n      r1_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc: 0.142\n      qwq_32b_best_layer: 30\n      qwq_32b_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.469\n      qwq_32b_static_acc: 0.809\n      qwq_32b_static_crossing_rate: 0.488\n      qwq_32b_static_lead_mean: -1.8\n      qwq_32b_static_precp_fpr: 0.155\n      qwq_32b_static_precision: 0.612\n      qwq_32b_static_safe_fpr: 0.374\n      qwq_32b_static_auroc: 0.827\n      qwq_32b_fisher_orig: 18.7\n      qwq_32b_fisher_pca3d: 0.98\n      qwq_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc: 0.151\n      n_traces_r1_32b: 209\n      n_traces_qwq_32b: 209\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires both 32B models to show HS pre-CP FPR in [0.12, 0.20] and precision in [0.55, 0.68]. The r1-8b baseline achieved precp_fpr=0.162 and precision=0.622. 32B models with 64 layers provide richer intermediate representations; the deeper architecture distributes safety-relevant features across more layers but with comparable discriminability at the optimal depth (~44%). QwQ-32B shows slightly weaker results because it's a different base architecture (Qwen2.5 vs DeepSeek) with potentially different safety training distributions, but still comfortably within the target range. Fisher ratio remains high in full space (~18-19) but collapses in PCA-3D (~1.0), confirming the minor-PC signal distribution is architectural, not model-specific. Layer sweep peaks at 28-30 (43-47% depth), consistent with r1-8b's 44% finding, suggesting middle-layer encoding of safety state is universal across reasoning model scales.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Static Probe Cross-Architecture Results\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Best Layer\", \"Depth%\", \"Acc\", \"Coverage\", \"Pre-CP FPR\", \"Precision\", \"AUROC\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (baseline)\", \"14/32\", \"44%\", \"0.817\", \"60.5%\", \"16.2%\", \"62.2%\", \"0.823\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"28/64\", \"44%\", \"0.824\", \"51.2%\", \"14.8%\", \"63.4%\", \"0.841\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"30/64\", \"47%\", \"0.809\", \"48.8%\", \"15.5%\", \"61.2%\", \"0.827\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 2a: Layer sweep accuracy curves for r1-32b and QwQ-32B (64 layers). Both show smooth unimodal peak at layers 28-30, mirroring r1-8b's peak at layer 14. Y-axis: probe accuracy, X-axis: layer index. Shaded region marks 40-50% depth band.\"\n      - \"Fig 2b: Fisher discriminant ratio vs PCA dimensionality (3, 10, 50, full). All 3 models show Fisher collapse from ~18 to ~1 at PCA-3D, confirming signal in minor principal components is universal.\"\n    analysis: \"Both 32B models replicate the core finding: static hidden-state probes achieve pre-CP FPR ~15% (well below text-based 27-36%), confirming that the precision advantage is not r1-8b-specific. The optimal layer at ~44% depth across all models suggests a universal depth-dependent encoding of safety reasoning state. Coverage is slightly lower on 32B (49-51% vs 60.5%) because longer 32B traces have more steps between safety reasoning segments, making static detection harder — this motivates exp-002's temporal window. Fisher collapse in PCA-3D replicates perfectly, establishing that TrajGuard-style methods will fail universally.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: \"Temporal LR W=15 on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Validate temporal coverage improvement holds cross-architecture.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_temporal_acc: 0.798\n      r1_32b_temporal_coverage: 0.581\n      r1_32b_temporal_coverage_delta: +0.069\n      r1_32b_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.142\n      r1_32b_temporal_precision: 0.601\n      r1_32b_temporal_lead: 5.8\n      r1_32b_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.355\n      r1_32b_temporal_auroc: 0.862\n      qwq_32b_temporal_acc: 0.781\n      qwq_32b_temporal_coverage: 0.558\n      qwq_32b_temporal_coverage_delta: +0.070\n      qwq_32b_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.149\n      qwq_32b_temporal_precision: 0.584\n      qwq_32b_temporal_lead: 5.2\n      qwq_32b_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.368\n      qwq_32b_temporal_auroc: 0.849\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires temporal_coverage_delta_32b in [0.04, 0.10]. r1-8b showed +4.6pp (60.5→65.1%). On 32B, the effect is slightly larger (+6.9-7.0pp) because longer 32B traces have more temporal context available and the W=15 window captures more informative transitions. The precision advantage of HS over text is maintained (precp_fpr stays ~14-15% vs text's expected 28-35%), and temporal context slightly improves it further (from 14.8→14.2% on r1-32b). This confirms Claim 2: temporal context is necessary regardless of architecture, and the hidden-state precision advantage is modality-specific and architecture-independent.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Temporal vs Static Probe (Cross-Architecture)\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Method\", \"Coverage\", \"Δ Coverage\", \"Pre-CP FPR\", \"Precision\", \"Lead\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"Static LR L14\", \"60.5%\", \"—\", \"16.2%\", \"62.2%\", \"+3.0\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"Temporal W=15\", \"65.1%\", \"+4.6pp\", \"16.0%\", \"58.3%\", \"+4.9\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"Static LR L28\", \"51.2%\", \"—\", \"14.8%\", \"63.4%\", \"-1.4\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"Temporal W=15\", \"58.1%\", \"+6.9pp\", \"14.2%\", \"60.1%\", \"+5.8\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"Static LR L30\", \"48.8%\", \"—\", \"15.5%\", \"61.2%\", \"-1.8\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"Temporal W=15\", \"55.8%\", \"+7.0pp\", \"14.9%\", \"58.4%\", \"+5.2\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3: Coverage improvement from temporal windowing across 3 models. Bar chart showing static vs temporal coverage for each model. All three show +5-7pp improvement, with 95% bootstrap CI error bars overlapping the common effect size range.\"\n      - \"Fig 4: Pre-CP FPR comparison (HS temporal vs text temporal) across 3 models. HS consistently at 14-16%, text at 27-35%. Clear separation with non-overlapping CIs.\"\n    analysis: \"Temporal windowing produces a consistent +5-7pp coverage gain across all three models, confirming that multi-step context is architecturally necessary for commitment detection. The 32B models show slightly larger temporal gains than r1-8b (+6.9-7.0pp vs +4.6pp), likely because their longer traces contain richer temporal structure within W=15. Crucially, the HS precision advantage is maintained: pre-CP FPR stays in 14-15% range vs text's 27-35%, establishing this as a modality-specific property independent of temporal context. The combined finding—temporal context helps both modalities equally, but HS is universally more precise—is the paper's strongest cross-architecture result.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cross-model transfer probes\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: \"Train probe on r1-8b (Llama-based, 4096-d), test on r1-32b/QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5-based, 5120-d) and vice versa. Projection head maps between dimensionalities.\"\n    expected_results:\n      llama_to_r1_32b_acc: 0.692\n      llama_to_qwq_32b_acc: 0.678\n      qwen_to_r1_8b_acc: 0.714\n      r1_32b_to_qwq_32b_acc: 0.743\n      qwq_32b_to_r1_32b_acc: 0.731\n      mean_cross_family_acc: 0.695\n      mean_within_family_acc: 0.737\n      random_baseline_acc: 0.50\n      projection_head_dim: 256\n      projection_head_params: 1310720\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires cross_transfer_acc in [0.62, 0.75]. Cross-family transfer (Llama↔Qwen2.5) achieves 67-71% because the safety commitment signal, while encoded differently in each architecture's hidden space, shares sufficient geometric structure that a learned linear projection can partially align them. The signal is not identical (hence not 80%+) but well above chance (50%), suggesting a shared 'safety geometry' across architectures. Within-family transfer (r1-32b↔QwQ-32B, both Qwen2.5-based) is higher (73-74%) because the base architecture already shares representational structure. The asymmetry (Qwen→Llama slightly better than Llama→Qwen) suggests Qwen2.5 models develop a more generalizable safety representation, possibly due to training data differences.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Cross-Model Transfer Matrix\"\n        columns: [\"Train Model\", \"Test Model\", \"Accuracy\", \"vs Native Probe\", \"vs Chance\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\", \"69.2%\", \"-13.2pp\", \"+19.2pp\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\", \"67.8%\", \"-13.1pp\", \"+17.8pp\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\", \"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"71.4%\", \"-10.3pp\", \"+21.4pp\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\", \"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"70.8%\", \"-10.9pp\", \"+20.8pp\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\", \"QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\", \"74.3%\", \"-6.6pp\", \"+24.3pp\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\", \"r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\", \"73.1%\", \"-9.3pp\", \"+23.1pp\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 5: Transfer accuracy heatmap (train × test). Diagonal blocked out (native). Warmer colors indicate higher transfer. Clear band of higher transfer within Qwen2.5 family. All cells above 0.62.\"\n      - \"Fig 5b: t-SNE of projected hidden states from all 3 models in shared 256-d space. Safety vs commitment steps show partial clustering across models, with model-specific clusters but overlapping decision boundaries.\"\n    analysis: \"Cross-model transfer achieves 67-74% accuracy, decisively above chance and suggesting shared safety signal geometry across architectures. This is a novel finding: despite different training procedures, model families, and representational spaces, the commitment point phenomenon leaves a geometrically similar trace in hidden states. The within-Qwen2.5 transfer being higher (73-74%) than cross-family (68-71%) is expected and informative—it suggests the signal has both a universal component (shared across all reasoning models) and an architecture-specific component. This result adds significant novelty: 'shared safety geometry' is a stronger claim than 'each model has detectable commitment points.'\"\n\n  - name: \"Lead time analysis across models\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: \"GRU and temporal probe lead time distributions on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Confirm unreliable pre-textual detection generalizes.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_temporal_lr_lead_mean: 5.8\n      r1_32b_temporal_lr_lead_median: 0\n      r1_32b_temporal_lr_positive_lead_rate: 0.42\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_mean: 6.2\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_median: 0\n      r1_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.45\n      r1_32b_gru_coverage: 0.81\n      r1_32b_gru_coverage_std: 0.09\n      qwq_32b_temporal_lr_lead_mean: 5.2\n      qwq_32b_temporal_lr_lead_median: -1\n      qwq_32b_temporal_lr_positive_lead_rate: 0.38\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean: 5.5\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_median: 0\n      qwq_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.41\n      qwq_32b_gru_coverage: 0.78\n      qwq_32b_gru_coverage_std: 0.11\n      three_model_gru_lead_median_range: [-1, 0]\n      three_model_positive_lead_rate_range: [0.35, 0.45]\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires gru_lead_mean_32b in [2.0, 8.0] and gru_lead_median_32b in [-1, 1]. The pattern replicates: positive mean (driven by a subset of traces with clear pre-commitment divergence) but median 0 or -1 (most detections occur at or slightly after the commitment point). This confirms that pre-textual detection unreliability is a fundamental property of reasoning models, not an r1-8b artifact. The 32B models have longer traces on average, which gives GRU more context but also more noise—the net effect is similar lead time distributions. GRU instability (coverage std 0.09-0.11 across seeds) also replicates, confirming the training signal's fundamental noisiness at this data scale.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: Lead Time Distribution Across Models\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Method\", \"Mean Lead\", \"Median Lead\", \"% Positive Lead\", \"Coverage\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"Temporal LR W=15\", \"+4.9\", \"0\", \"39%\", \"65.1%\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"GRU multi-7L\", \"+4.7\", \"0\", \"39%\", \"76-93%*\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"Temporal LR W=15\", \"+5.8\", \"0\", \"42%\", \"58.1%\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"GRU multi-7L\", \"+6.2\", \"0\", \"45%\", \"81%±9%*\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"Temporal LR W=15\", \"+5.2\", \"-1\", \"38%\", \"55.8%\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"GRU multi-7L\", \"+5.5\", \"0\", \"41%\", \"78%±11%*\"]\n      - name: \"* Multi-seed mean±std\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 6: Lead time distribution histograms (3 models × 2 methods). All show right-skewed distributions centered near 0, with long positive tails (up to +30 steps) but substantial mass at negative values. Shape is consistent across models.\"\n      - \"Fig 6b: CDF of detection delay. Oracle CDF rises steeply from +20-40 steps, while all probe CDFs cross 50% near lead=0. The gap between oracle and best probe is consistent across all 3 models (~25-30 steps at 50th percentile).\"\n    analysis: \"Lead time unreliability replicates perfectly across architectures. The signature pattern—positive mean, zero/negative median—appears on all 3 models, establishing this as a fundamental property of reasoning model safety signals. The oracle gap (100% early detection vs ~40% for best probes) is consistent, indicating that hidden states encode current safety state accurately but poorly predict the future trajectory. GRU coverage instability also replicates (std 9-11% across seeds), confirming this reflects fundamental signal noise rather than model-specific limitations. This three-model consistency strengthens Claim 3 from 'observed on r1-8b' to 'universal property of reasoning model commitment dynamics.'\"\n\n  - name: \"TrajGuard Mahalanobis baseline (all 3 models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: \"Mahalanobis distance baseline on 32B models. Expected to fail due to signal in minor PCs.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_md_best_crossing_rate: 0.279\n      r1_32b_md_best_config: \"pca_64_p75\"\n      r1_32b_md_best_lead: 14.8\n      r1_32b_safe_md_mean: 7.12\n      r1_32b_jb_pre_md_mean: 7.18\n      r1_32b_jb_post_md_mean: 7.08\n      r1_32b_delta_max: 0.019\n      qwq_32b_md_best_crossing_rate: 0.256\n      qwq_32b_md_best_config: \"pca_128_p75\"\n      qwq_32b_md_best_lead: 15.2\n      qwq_32b_safe_md_mean: 7.45\n      qwq_32b_jb_pre_md_mean: 7.51\n      qwq_32b_jb_post_md_mean: 7.39\n      qwq_32b_delta_max: 0.022\n      verdict: \"Fails on all 3 models. MD cannot distinguish safe from jailbreak traces.\"\n    result_rationale: \"The PCA analysis in exp-001 confirms Fisher collapse in 3D holds on 32B models (Fisher drops from ~18-19 to ~1.0 in PCA-3D). Since TrajGuard relies on Mahalanobis distance in the principal subspace, and the safety signal lives in minor components, the method fundamentally cannot separate safety states. The 32B results are slightly worse than r1-8b (crossing rate 26-28% vs 30%) because the 5120-d space distributes the signal across even more minor components. The near-zero delta between safe/jb-pre/jb-post MD values confirms that in the covariance-normalized space, safety commitment is invisible. This universally validates the 'existing detectors fail' claim.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: TrajGuard/Mahalanobis Failure Across Models\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Best Config\", \"Crossing Rate (k=5)\", \"MD(safe)\", \"MD(jb-pre)\", \"MD(jb-post)\", \"Max Δ\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"pca_64_p75\", \"30.2%\", \"6.66\", \"6.73\", \"6.64\", \"0.023\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"pca_64_p75\", \"27.9%\", \"7.12\", \"7.18\", \"7.08\", \"0.019\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"pca_128_p75\", \"25.6%\", \"7.45\", \"7.51\", \"7.39\", \"0.022\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 7: MD trajectory plots for safe vs jailbreak traces (3 models). Completely overlapping distributions in all cases. Annotated commitment points show no MD inflection.\"\n    analysis: \"Universal failure of geometric methods across all 3 models and architectures. The negligible delta between safe and jailbreak MD values (<0.025 across all models) confirms that in the variance-dominated subspace, safety commitment is undetectable. This is the empirical backing for our theoretical argument: safety signals in minor PCs require supervised probing, not unsupervised geometric methods. The consistency of failure across Llama-based and Qwen2.5-based models makes this a strong negative result about the entire class of TrajGuard-style detectors applied to reasoning model safety.\"\n\n  - name: \"Bootstrap confidence intervals + threshold-independent metrics\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: \"1000 bootstrap resamples on all key metrics across all 3 models. AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF, cross-validation variance.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_hs_temporal_coverage_ci: \"[0.442, 0.698]\"\n      r1_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr_ci: \"[0.082, 0.215]\"\n      r1_32b_hs_temporal_precision_ci: \"[0.498, 0.712]\"\n      qwq_32b_hs_temporal_coverage_ci: \"[0.419, 0.681]\"\n      qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr_ci: \"[0.091, 0.224]\"\n      qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precision_ci: \"[0.479, 0.695]\"\n      pooled_3model_hs_vs_text_precp_fpr_p: 0.003\n      pooled_3model_temporal_vs_static_coverage_p: 0.028\n      pooled_hs_precision_advantage_ci: \"[0.08, 0.18]\"\n      cross_model_effect_consistency_I2: 0.12\n      r1_32b_auroc_hs_temporal: 0.862\n      r1_32b_auroc_text_temporal: 0.781\n      qwq_32b_auroc_hs_temporal: 0.849\n      qwq_32b_auroc_text_temporal: 0.769\n    result_rationale: \"With n=43 test traces per model, individual CIs are wide (as observed in r1-8b exp-006 results). However, pooling across 3 models (n≈129 effective traces) narrows CIs substantially. The pooled HS-vs-text pre-CP FPR comparison achieves p=0.003 because the effect is large (ratio ≥1.7x) and consistent across all 3 models. The low I² statistic (0.12) indicates minimal heterogeneity—the effect size is remarkably consistent across architectures, which is the paper's strongest statistical argument. Individual per-model comparisons may not reach significance (p=0.05-0.15), but the meta-analytic combination across 3 models is decisive.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: Pooled Bootstrap Results (3 Models)\"\n        columns: [\"Comparison\", \"Effect Size\", \"95% CI\", \"p-value\", \"I²\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"HS vs Text pre-CP FPR\", \"-14.2pp\", \"[-8.1, -17.8]\", \"0.003\", \"0.12\"]\n          - [\"HS vs Text precision\", \"+12.4pp\", \"[+7.9, +16.1]\", \"0.001\", \"0.08\"]\n          - [\"Temporal vs Static coverage\", \"+5.8pp\", \"[+0.6, +10.4]\", \"0.028\", \"0.21\"]\n          - [\"GRU vs LR lead time\", \"+2.1 steps\", \"[-1.2, +5.8]\", \"0.14\", \"0.31\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 8: Forest plot of HS precision advantage across 3 models. Each model's point estimate with CI, plus pooled diamond. All point estimates positive (10-13pp), pooled CI excludes zero.\"\n      - \"Fig 8b: PR curves for HS temporal vs text temporal on each model. HS curve dominates text curve across all recall levels, consistently across all 3 models.\"\n      - \"Fig 8c: AUROC comparison bar chart (3 models × 2 modalities). HS temporal consistently 7-8pp above text temporal.\"\n    analysis: \"The three-model pooled analysis resolves the statistical power concern that plagued single-model results. While individual per-model p-values are marginal (0.05-0.15), the pooled comparison with I²=0.12 (minimal heterogeneity) achieves p=0.003 for the HS precision advantage. This is the correct statistical framing for the paper: consistent effect across multiple independent models, not single-model significance. The AUROC advantage (8-9pp) is threshold-independent confirmation that HS monitoring is decisively superior. Temporal vs static coverage reaches p=0.028 pooled, confirming Claim 2. GRU vs LR lead time doesn't reach significance (p=0.14), honestly acknowledging the noisy lead time signal.\"\n\n  - name: \"Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: \"Window size sweep on all 3 models to find optimal W and show diminishing returns.\"\n    expected_results:\n      optimal_w_r1_8b: 15\n      optimal_w_r1_32b: 15\n      optimal_w_qwq_32b: 20\n      coverage_at_w1_mean: 0.49\n      coverage_at_w5_mean: 0.52\n      coverage_at_w10_mean: 0.56\n      coverage_at_w15_mean: 0.60\n      coverage_at_w20_mean: 0.61\n      coverage_at_w25_mean: 0.60\n      diminishing_returns_elbow: \"W=10-15\"\n      precp_fpr_stable_across_w: true\n      precp_fpr_range_all_w: \"[0.13, 0.17]\"\n    result_rationale: \"Temporal context shows diminishing returns because the commitment point is a relatively local phenomenon (safety reasoning typically ceases within a 10-20 step window). W=15 captures the critical transition period; beyond that, earlier steps add noise without information. The optimal W is consistent across models (15-20) because reasoning model safety dynamics operate on similar timescales regardless of model size. Crucially, pre-CP FPR remains stable across all W values (13-17%), confirming that the HS precision advantage is not an artifact of window size selection—it's a property of the signal modality.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A1: Window Size Ablation\"\n        columns: [\"W\", \"r1-8b Cov\", \"r1-32b Cov\", \"QwQ Cov\", \"Mean Pre-CP FPR\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"1 (static)\", \"60.5%\", \"51.2%\", \"48.8%\", \"15.5%\"]\n          - [\"3\", \"61.2%\", \"52.4%\", \"49.5%\", \"15.2%\"]\n          - [\"5\", \"62.3%\", \"53.8%\", \"51.2%\", \"14.8%\"]\n          - [\"10\", \"63.8%\", \"56.1%\", \"54.0%\", \"14.5%\"]\n          - [\"15\", \"65.1%\", \"58.1%\", \"55.8%\", \"14.7%\"]\n          - [\"20\", \"64.8%\", \"58.4%\", \"56.9%\", \"15.1%\"]\n          - [\"25\", \"64.2%\", \"57.6%\", \"55.4%\", \"15.8%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A1: Coverage vs window size (3 models). Monotone increase until W=15-20, then plateau/slight decrease. Elbow at W=10-15.\"\n      - \"Fig A1b: Pre-CP FPR vs window size (3 models). Nearly flat lines across all W values, confirming FPR independence from temporal context width.\"\n    analysis: \"The diminishing returns curve peaks at W=15-20 across all models, with coverage gains saturating after the elbow. This is consistent with commitment dynamics: the critical transition from safety reasoning to harmful execution occurs within a ~15-step window, and additional context beyond that provides no incremental signal. The stability of pre-CP FPR across W values is important methodologically—it means the precision advantage of HS monitoring is robust to hyperparameter choices. For the paper, W=15 is the default recommendation, with acknowledgment that W=20 may be marginally better for models with longer traces.\"\n\n  - name: \"Layer selection ablation (all layers, 32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: \"Full layer sweep with temporal probe on 32B models. Confirm middle-layer finding generalizes.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_temporal_best_layer: 28\n      r1_32b_temporal_best_acc: 0.798\n      r1_32b_temporal_layer_range_within_2pp: \"[24, 34]\"\n      r1_32b_early_layers_1_10_mean_acc: 0.62\n      r1_32b_late_layers_55_64_mean_acc: 0.71\n      qwq_32b_temporal_best_layer: 30\n      qwq_32b_temporal_best_acc: 0.781\n      qwq_32b_temporal_layer_range_within_2pp: \"[26, 36]\"\n      middle_layer_advantage_over_last: \"+8-10pp\"\n      depth_correlation_with_r1_8b: 0.94\n    result_rationale: \"The middle-layer peak generalizes because of how transformer representations evolve: early layers encode syntactic/positional information, middle layers encode semantic/behavioral states (including safety reasoning state), and late layers specialize for next-token prediction. The safety commitment signal is a behavioral state property, hence middle-layer encoding. The peak being at ~44-47% depth (not 50%) is consistent across all models, suggesting the safety information is processed in the first half of the network and progressively refined. The broad plateau (±5 layers within 2pp of peak) means the exact layer choice is not critical—a practical advantage for deployment.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A2: Layer Selection (Temporal Probe)\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Best Layer\", \"Depth%\", \"Peak Acc\", \"Plateau Width (±2pp)\", \"Last Layer Acc\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (32L)\", \"14\", \"44%\", \"0.848\", \"L10-L18\", \"0.761\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (64L)\", \"28\", \"44%\", \"0.798\", \"L24-L34\", \"0.712\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (64L)\", \"30\", \"47%\", \"0.781\", \"L26-L36\", \"0.698\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A2: Temporal probe accuracy vs layer (all layers, 3 models). X-axis normalized to depth percentage. All three curves peak at 44-47% depth with similar shape—inverted U with broad plateau. Includes shaded ±1 std band from CV folds.\"\n    analysis: \"The middle-layer finding is universal across architectures and scales. The depth-normalized accuracy curves are nearly identical (correlation 0.94 between r1-8b and 32B models), confirming that safety state encoding follows the same depth-dependent pattern regardless of total layer count. The broad plateau (10+ layers within 2pp of peak) is practically important: it means the method is robust to layer selection, and a simple heuristic (select layer at 44% depth) works across models without per-model tuning.\"\n\n  - name: \"16-label taxonomy supervision\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: \"Use fine-grained Type 1/2/3/4 labels instead of binary commitment for richer training signal.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_16label_coverage: 0.674\n      r1_8b_16label_precp_fpr: 0.151\n      r1_8b_16label_precision: 0.641\n      r1_8b_16label_lead: 5.4\n      r1_8b_improvement_over_binary: \"+2.3pp coverage, +2pp precision\"\n      r1_32b_16label_coverage: 0.605\n      r1_32b_16label_precp_fpr: 0.138\n      r1_32b_16label_precision: 0.618\n      transition_type_most_informative: \"Type 3 (safety-to-harmful transition)\"\n      diminishing_returns_vs_binary: \"modest\"\n    result_rationale: \"Fine-grained labels provide modest improvement because the binary commitment signal already captures the primary discriminative information. The 16-label taxonomy helps most at the transition boundaries (Type 3: safety→harmful), where the additional supervision helps the probe distinguish 'approaching commitment' from 'already committed.' However, the improvement is modest (+2-3pp) because the temporal window already captures this transition implicitly. The main value is diagnostic: confirming which label types are most informative for detection timing. This is a P1 experiment because the improvement doesn't change the paper's conclusions.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A3: Binary vs 16-Label Supervision\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Supervision\", \"Coverage\", \"Pre-CP FPR\", \"Precision\", \"Lead\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"Binary W=15\", \"65.1%\", \"16.0%\", \"58.3%\", \"+4.9\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"16-label W=15\", \"67.4%\", \"15.1%\", \"64.1%\", \"+5.4\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"Binary W=15\", \"58.1%\", \"14.2%\", \"60.1%\", \"+5.8\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"16-label W=15\", \"60.5%\", \"13.8%\", \"61.8%\", \"+6.1\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A3: Per-label-type probe confidence trajectories showing which transitions are most detectable. Type 3 (safety→harmful) shows sharpest confidence increase, consistent with commitment point definition.\"\n    analysis: \"16-label supervision provides modest but consistent improvement (+2-3pp coverage and precision). The improvement is largest for traces where the binary label masks a gradual transition through multiple safety states before commitment. However, the gains are small enough that binary supervision remains the recommended default for simplicity. The per-type analysis confirms that Type 3 transitions are most informative, which aligns with the commitment point definition and provides theoretical validation.\"\n\n  - name: \"Oracle decomposition\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: \"Analyze what oracle labels encode that hidden states don't. Decompose the +31-step oracle advantage.\"\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_advantage_source_breakdown:\n        future_label_pattern: \"62%\"\n        multi_step_trajectory: \"28%\"\n        global_trace_context: \"10%\"\n      oracle_K1_early_rate: 0.837\n      probe_best_early_rate: 0.42\n      gap_attributable_to_future_info: \"72%\"\n      gap_attributable_to_training_signal: \"28%\"\n      key_insight: \"Oracle uses FUTURE labels; probes can only use PAST hidden states. The commitment point is fundamentally unpredictable from pre-commitment representations because the model has not yet 'decided.'\"\n    result_rationale: \"The oracle achieves 100% detection with +31 mean lead because it has access to future labels (it knows the commitment point by definition and can look ahead K steps). The probe can only access past/current hidden states, and the model's representations before commitment encode the CURRENT safety reasoning state, not the FUTURE trajectory. Decomposition shows that 62% of the oracle advantage comes from future label information (which is definitionally unavailable to any real-time method), 28% from multi-step trajectory shape (partially capturable by GRU but noisy), and 10% from global trace context. This establishes the gap as principled: no method operating in real-time can close >72% of the oracle gap.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 9: Oracle Advantage Decomposition\"\n        columns: [\"Information Source\", \"% of Oracle Advantage\", \"Accessible to Probes?\", \"Method Exploiting It\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Future labels (K-step lookahead)\", \"62%\", \"No (definitional)\", \"None possible\"]\n          - [\"Multi-step trajectory shape\", \"28%\", \"Partially (noisy)\", \"GRU/temporal\"]\n          - [\"Global trace context (trace length, topic)\", \"10%\", \"Yes\", \"Features, but low signal\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 9: Ablation showing oracle performance as information sources are progressively removed. Removing future labels drops oracle from 100% to ~38% early detection (matching probe performance). This gap is irreducible.\"\n    analysis: \"The oracle decomposition establishes that pre-textual detection is fundamentally limited by information asymmetry: the commitment point is a future event whose timing cannot be reliably predicted from current representations. The model's hidden states before commitment encode ongoing safety reasoning but do NOT encode when that reasoning will terminate. This is the paper's key theoretical insight for Claim 3: unreliable pre-textual detection is not a method limitation but an information-theoretic constraint. Future work would need to identify pre-commitment signals that are predictive of commitment timing, which our analysis suggests are either absent or extremely subtle.\"\n\n  - name: \"OpenThinker-7B (4th model, scale control)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: \"Qwen2.5-7B-based reasoning model. 2×2 matrix: Llama×Qwen2.5 × 7-8B×32B.\"\n    expected_results:\n      openthinker_best_layer: 14\n      openthinker_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.50\n      openthinker_static_acc: 0.795\n      openthinker_static_precp_fpr: 0.172\n      openthinker_static_precision: 0.589\n      openthinker_temporal_coverage: 0.628\n      openthinker_temporal_coverage_delta: +0.058\n      openthinker_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.165\n      openthinker_temporal_precision: 0.571\n      openthinker_gru_lead_mean: 4.1\n      openthinker_gru_lead_median: 0\n      scale_effect_7b_vs_32b_precision_delta: \"-2.5pp\"\n      architecture_effect_llama_vs_qwen_precision_delta: \"-1.8pp\"\n    result_rationale: \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base) fills the 2×2 design matrix. Its results should be intermediate: same architecture family as QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5) but same scale as r1-8b (7-8B parameters). If scale dominates architecture, OpenThinker should match r1-8b; if architecture dominates, it should match QwQ-32B. In the best tier, both factors contribute modestly but neither dominates—the signal quality is primarily determined by the reasoning training procedure, not base model properties. Pre-CP FPR at 0.172 is within the [0.12, 0.20] range, confirming the HS advantage holds at all tested scales and architectures.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A4: 2×2 Architecture×Scale Matrix\"\n        columns: [\"Architecture\", \"Scale\", \"Model\", \"Pre-CP FPR\", \"Precision\", \"Temporal Δ\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Llama\", \"8B\", \"r1-8b\", \"16.0%\", \"58.3%\", \"+4.6pp\"]\n          - [\"Qwen2.5\", \"7B\", \"OpenThinker-7B\", \"16.5%\", \"57.1%\", \"+5.8pp\"]\n          - [\"Qwen2.5\", \"32B\", \"r1-32b\", \"14.2%\", \"60.1%\", \"+6.9pp\"]\n          - [\"Qwen2.5\", \"32B\", \"QwQ-32B\", \"14.9%\", \"58.4%\", \"+7.0pp\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A4: 2×2 interaction plot. X-axis: scale (7-8B vs 32B). Lines: architecture (Llama, Qwen2.5). Near-parallel lines indicate additive effects without strong interaction. Both factors contribute ~2pp to precision.\"\n    analysis: \"The 2×2 matrix reveals that both architecture and scale contribute modestly to probe quality, but neither is dominant—the primary determinant is the presence of reasoning training. The near-parallel lines in the interaction plot indicate no architecture×scale interaction, meaning the findings generalize cleanly. This result strengthens the paper's universality claim: safety commitment detection works consistently across the tested space of reasoning models. Scale provides a modest 2-3pp precision advantage (likely due to richer representations), and architecture contributes similarly.\"\n\n  - name: \"GRU/Attention probe on 32B models\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: \"GRU h=256 multi-layer on 32B models. Verify instability pattern and 'simplicity is optimal' conclusion.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_gru_coverage_mean: 0.81\n      r1_32b_gru_coverage_std: 0.09\n      r1_32b_gru_coverage_range: \"[0.72, 0.91]\"\n      r1_32b_gru_precp_fpr: 0.195\n      r1_32b_gru_precision: 0.612\n      r1_32b_gru_safe_fpr: 0.58\n      qwq_32b_gru_coverage_mean: 0.78\n      qwq_32b_gru_coverage_std: 0.11\n      qwq_32b_gru_coverage_range: \"[0.67, 0.89]\"\n      qwq_32b_gru_precp_fpr: 0.208\n      qwq_32b_gru_precision: 0.591\n      qwq_32b_gru_safe_fpr: 0.61\n      gru_vs_lr_precision_advantage: \"negligible (+1-3pp)\"\n      gru_vs_lr_safe_fpr_penalty: \"+20pp\"\n      verdict: \"GRU trades precision for coverage; LR remains preferred for pre-commitment monitoring.\"\n    result_rationale: \"GRU instability replicates on 32B models because it's driven by training data scarcity (n=125 training traces) and the fundamentally noisy commitment signal, not model-specific properties. The std of 9-11% across seeds confirms that GRU's capacity exceeds what the training data can stably supervise. On 32B, GRU achieves marginally higher coverage (78-81%) but at the cost of substantially higher safe FPR (58-61% vs LR's 35-37%). This confirms the 'simplicity is optimal' narrative: for the critical pre-commitment detection task, temporal LR provides the best precision/FPR tradeoff, while GRU is useful only if coverage is prioritized over precision. The paper frames this as: 'more complex models can detect more commitment points but at unacceptable false positive cost.'\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A5: GRU vs LR on 32B Models (5-seed mean±std)\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Method\", \"Coverage\", \"Pre-CP FPR\", \"Precision\", \"Safe FPR\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"Temporal LR W=15\", \"58.1%±3.2%\", \"14.2%\", \"60.1%\", \"35.5%\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"GRU multi-7L\", \"81%±9%\", \"19.5%\", \"61.2%\", \"58%\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"Temporal LR W=15\", \"55.8%±3.8%\", \"14.9%\", \"58.4%\", \"36.8%\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"GRU multi-7L\", \"78%±11%\", \"20.8%\", \"59.1%\", \"61%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A5: Coverage vs safe FPR tradeoff plot (Pareto front). LR methods cluster in the low-FPR/moderate-coverage region; GRU methods in the high-coverage/high-FPR region. No method achieves both high coverage AND low FPR.\"\n      - \"Fig A5b: GRU coverage distribution across 5 seeds (violin plots, 3 models). Wide distributions confirm instability is universal.\"\n    analysis: \"GRU instability replicating on 32B models confirms it's a fundamental signal property: with n=125 training traces, the GRU's capacity leads to overfitting to spurious temporal patterns that vary across initializations. The coverage-vs-FPR tradeoff is universal: GRU gains ~20pp coverage over LR but pays ~22pp in safe FPR. For deployment scenarios prioritizing precision in the pre-commitment window (the paper's primary framing), temporal LR remains optimal. This strengthens the 'simplicity is optimal for this task' conclusion—not because complex models can't learn, but because the training data is insufficient to stably train them.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13002,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13002},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92317e92-59ba-40d8-b9f8-d311cd7b1e86","uuid":"201380ea-b37e-4c3f-8476-70b137cf8dc5"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":259244,"duration_api_ms":260444,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: \"Original framing intact. Three-model validation (r1-8b, r1-32b, QwQ-32B) makes all 3 claims robust. Cross-transfer result adds bonus novelty ('shared safety geometry across architectures'). Paper title emphasizes the systematic evaluation + cross-architecture consistency. Story: hidden states universally encode safety commitment better than text, temporal context universally necessary, pre-textual detection universally unreliable — this is a property of reasoning models, not one model.\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Cross-architecture static probe (r1-32b + QwQ-32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: \"Layer sweep on 64-layer 32B models, static LR probe evaluation at best layer. Validates Claim 1 generalizes beyond r1-8b.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_best_layer: 28\n      r1_32b_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.4375\n      r1_32b_static_acc: 0.824\n      r1_32b_static_crossing_rate: 0.512\n      r1_32b_static_lead_mean: -1.4\n      r1_32b_static_precp_fpr: 0.148\n      r1_32b_static_precision: 0.634\n      r1_32b_static_safe_fpr: 0.362\n      r1_32b_static_auroc: 0.841\n      r1_32b_fisher_orig: 19.3\n      r1_32b_fisher_pca3d: 1.04\n      r1_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc: 0.142\n      qwq_32b_best_layer: 30\n      qwq_32b_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.469\n      qwq_32b_static_acc: 0.809\n      qwq_32b_static_crossing_rate: 0.488\n      qwq_32b_static_lead_mean: -1.8\n      qwq_32b_static_precp_fpr: 0.155\n      qwq_32b_static_precision: 0.612\n      qwq_32b_static_safe_fpr: 0.374\n      qwq_32b_static_auroc: 0.827\n      qwq_32b_fisher_orig: 18.7\n      qwq_32b_fisher_pca3d: 0.98\n      qwq_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc: 0.151\n      n_traces_r1_32b: 209\n      n_traces_qwq_32b: 209\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires both 32B models to show HS pre-CP FPR in [0.12, 0.20] and precision in [0.55, 0.68]. The r1-8b baseline achieved precp_fpr=0.162 and precision=0.622. 32B models with 64 layers provide richer intermediate representations; the deeper architecture distributes safety-relevant features across more layers but with comparable discriminability at the optimal depth (~44%). QwQ-32B shows slightly weaker results because it's a different base architecture (Qwen2.5 vs DeepSeek) with potentially different safety training distributions, but still comfortably within the target range. Fisher ratio remains high in full space (~18-19) but collapses in PCA-3D (~1.0), confirming the minor-PC signal distribution is architectural, not model-specific. Layer sweep peaks at 28-30 (43-47% depth), consistent with r1-8b's 44% finding, suggesting middle-layer encoding of safety state is universal across reasoning model scales.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Static Probe Cross-Architecture Results\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Best Layer\", \"Depth%\", \"Acc\", \"Coverage\", \"Pre-CP FPR\", \"Precision\", \"AUROC\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (baseline)\", \"14/32\", \"44%\", \"0.817\", \"60.5%\", \"16.2%\", \"62.2%\", \"0.823\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"28/64\", \"44%\", \"0.824\", \"51.2%\", \"14.8%\", \"63.4%\", \"0.841\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"30/64\", \"47%\", \"0.809\", \"48.8%\", \"15.5%\", \"61.2%\", \"0.827\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 2a: Layer sweep accuracy curves for r1-32b and QwQ-32B (64 layers). Both show smooth unimodal peak at layers 28-30, mirroring r1-8b's peak at layer 14. Y-axis: probe accuracy, X-axis: layer index. Shaded region marks 40-50% depth band.\"\n      - \"Fig 2b: Fisher discriminant ratio vs PCA dimensionality (3, 10, 50, full). All 3 models show Fisher collapse from ~18 to ~1 at PCA-3D, confirming signal in minor principal components is universal.\"\n    analysis: \"Both 32B models replicate the core finding: static hidden-state probes achieve pre-CP FPR ~15% (well below text-based 27-36%), confirming that the precision advantage is not r1-8b-specific. The optimal layer at ~44% depth across all models suggests a universal depth-dependent encoding of safety reasoning state. Coverage is slightly lower on 32B (49-51% vs 60.5%) because longer 32B traces have more steps between safety reasoning segments, making static detection harder — this motivates exp-002's temporal window. Fisher collapse in PCA-3D replicates perfectly, establishing that TrajGuard-style methods will fail universally.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: \"Temporal LR W=15 on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Validate temporal coverage improvement holds cross-architecture.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_temporal_acc: 0.798\n      r1_32b_temporal_coverage: 0.581\n      r1_32b_temporal_coverage_delta: +0.069\n      r1_32b_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.142\n      r1_32b_temporal_precision: 0.601\n      r1_32b_temporal_lead: 5.8\n      r1_32b_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.355\n      r1_32b_temporal_auroc: 0.862\n      qwq_32b_temporal_acc: 0.781\n      qwq_32b_temporal_coverage: 0.558\n      qwq_32b_temporal_coverage_delta: +0.070\n      qwq_32b_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.149\n      qwq_32b_temporal_precision: 0.584\n      qwq_32b_temporal_lead: 5.2\n      qwq_32b_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.368\n      qwq_32b_temporal_auroc: 0.849\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires temporal_coverage_delta_32b in [0.04, 0.10]. r1-8b showed +4.6pp (60.5→65.1%). On 32B, the effect is slightly larger (+6.9-7.0pp) because longer 32B traces have more temporal context available and the W=15 window captures more informative transitions. The precision advantage of HS over text is maintained (precp_fpr stays ~14-15% vs text's expected 28-35%), and temporal context slightly improves it further (from 14.8→14.2% on r1-32b). This confirms Claim 2: temporal context is necessary regardless of architecture, and the hidden-state precision advantage is modality-specific and architecture-independent.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Temporal vs Static Probe (Cross-Architecture)\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Method\", \"Coverage\", \"Δ Coverage\", \"Pre-CP FPR\", \"Precision\", \"Lead\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"Static LR L14\", \"60.5%\", \"—\", \"16.2%\", \"62.2%\", \"+3.0\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"Temporal W=15\", \"65.1%\", \"+4.6pp\", \"16.0%\", \"58.3%\", \"+4.9\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"Static LR L28\", \"51.2%\", \"—\", \"14.8%\", \"63.4%\", \"-1.4\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"Temporal W=15\", \"58.1%\", \"+6.9pp\", \"14.2%\", \"60.1%\", \"+5.8\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"Static LR L30\", \"48.8%\", \"—\", \"15.5%\", \"61.2%\", \"-1.8\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"Temporal W=15\", \"55.8%\", \"+7.0pp\", \"14.9%\", \"58.4%\", \"+5.2\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3: Coverage improvement from temporal windowing across 3 models. Bar chart showing static vs temporal coverage for each model. All three show +5-7pp improvement, with 95% bootstrap CI error bars overlapping the common effect size range.\"\n      - \"Fig 4: Pre-CP FPR comparison (HS temporal vs text temporal) across 3 models. HS consistently at 14-16%, text at 27-35%. Clear separation with non-overlapping CIs.\"\n    analysis: \"Temporal windowing produces a consistent +5-7pp coverage gain across all three models, confirming that multi-step context is architecturally necessary for commitment detection. The 32B models show slightly larger temporal gains than r1-8b (+6.9-7.0pp vs +4.6pp), likely because their longer traces contain richer temporal structure within W=15. Crucially, the HS precision advantage is maintained: pre-CP FPR stays in 14-15% range vs text's 27-35%, establishing this as a modality-specific property independent of temporal context. The combined finding—temporal context helps both modalities equally, but HS is universally more precise—is the paper's strongest cross-architecture result.\"\n\n  - name: \"Cross-model transfer probes\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: \"Train probe on r1-8b (Llama-based, 4096-d), test on r1-32b/QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5-based, 5120-d) and vice versa. Projection head maps between dimensionalities.\"\n    expected_results:\n      llama_to_r1_32b_acc: 0.692\n      llama_to_qwq_32b_acc: 0.678\n      qwen_to_r1_8b_acc: 0.714\n      r1_32b_to_qwq_32b_acc: 0.743\n      qwq_32b_to_r1_32b_acc: 0.731\n      mean_cross_family_acc: 0.695\n      mean_within_family_acc: 0.737\n      random_baseline_acc: 0.50\n      projection_head_dim: 256\n      projection_head_params: 1310720\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires cross_transfer_acc in [0.62, 0.75]. Cross-family transfer (Llama↔Qwen2.5) achieves 67-71% because the safety commitment signal, while encoded differently in each architecture's hidden space, shares sufficient geometric structure that a learned linear projection can partially align them. The signal is not identical (hence not 80%+) but well above chance (50%), suggesting a shared 'safety geometry' across architectures. Within-family transfer (r1-32b↔QwQ-32B, both Qwen2.5-based) is higher (73-74%) because the base architecture already shares representational structure. The asymmetry (Qwen→Llama slightly better than Llama→Qwen) suggests Qwen2.5 models develop a more generalizable safety representation, possibly due to training data differences.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Cross-Model Transfer Matrix\"\n        columns: [\"Train Model\", \"Test Model\", \"Accuracy\", \"vs Native Probe\", \"vs Chance\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\", \"69.2%\", \"-13.2pp\", \"+19.2pp\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\", \"67.8%\", \"-13.1pp\", \"+17.8pp\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\", \"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"71.4%\", \"-10.3pp\", \"+21.4pp\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\", \"r1-8b (Llama)\", \"70.8%\", \"-10.9pp\", \"+20.8pp\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\", \"QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\", \"74.3%\", \"-6.6pp\", \"+24.3pp\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\", \"r1-32b (Qwen2.5)\", \"73.1%\", \"-9.3pp\", \"+23.1pp\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 5: Transfer accuracy heatmap (train × test). Diagonal blocked out (native). Warmer colors indicate higher transfer. Clear band of higher transfer within Qwen2.5 family. All cells above 0.62.\"\n      - \"Fig 5b: t-SNE of projected hidden states from all 3 models in shared 256-d space. Safety vs commitment steps show partial clustering across models, with model-specific clusters but overlapping decision boundaries.\"\n    analysis: \"Cross-model transfer achieves 67-74% accuracy, decisively above chance and suggesting shared safety signal geometry across architectures. This is a novel finding: despite different training procedures, model families, and representational spaces, the commitment point phenomenon leaves a geometrically similar trace in hidden states. The within-Qwen2.5 transfer being higher (73-74%) than cross-family (68-71%) is expected and informative—it suggests the signal has both a universal component (shared across all reasoning models) and an architecture-specific component. This result adds significant novelty: 'shared safety geometry' is a stronger claim than 'each model has detectable commitment points.'\"\n\n  - name: \"Lead time analysis across models\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: \"GRU and temporal probe lead time distributions on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Confirm unreliable pre-textual detection generalizes.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_temporal_lr_lead_mean: 5.8\n      r1_32b_temporal_lr_lead_median: 0\n      r1_32b_temporal_lr_positive_lead_rate: 0.42\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_mean: 6.2\n      r1_32b_gru_lead_median: 0\n      r1_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.45\n      r1_32b_gru_coverage: 0.81\n      r1_32b_gru_coverage_std: 0.09\n      qwq_32b_temporal_lr_lead_mean: 5.2\n      qwq_32b_temporal_lr_lead_median: -1\n      qwq_32b_temporal_lr_positive_lead_rate: 0.38\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean: 5.5\n      qwq_32b_gru_lead_median: 0\n      qwq_32b_gru_positive_lead_rate: 0.41\n      qwq_32b_gru_coverage: 0.78\n      qwq_32b_gru_coverage_std: 0.11\n      three_model_gru_lead_median_range: [-1, 0]\n      three_model_positive_lead_rate_range: [0.35, 0.45]\n    result_rationale: \"Best tier requires gru_lead_mean_32b in [2.0, 8.0] and gru_lead_median_32b in [-1, 1]. The pattern replicates: positive mean (driven by a subset of traces with clear pre-commitment divergence) but median 0 or -1 (most detections occur at or slightly after the commitment point). This confirms that pre-textual detection unreliability is a fundamental property of reasoning models, not an r1-8b artifact. The 32B models have longer traces on average, which gives GRU more context but also more noise—the net effect is similar lead time distributions. GRU instability (coverage std 0.09-0.11 across seeds) also replicates, confirming the training signal's fundamental noisiness at this data scale.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: Lead Time Distribution Across Models\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Method\", \"Mean Lead\", \"Median Lead\", \"% Positive Lead\", \"Coverage\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"Temporal LR W=15\", \"+4.9\", \"0\", \"39%\", \"65.1%\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"GRU multi-7L\", \"+4.7\", \"0\", \"39%\", \"76-93%*\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"Temporal LR W=15\", \"+5.8\", \"0\", \"42%\", \"58.1%\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"GRU multi-7L\", \"+6.2\", \"0\", \"45%\", \"81%±9%*\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"Temporal LR W=15\", \"+5.2\", \"-1\", \"38%\", \"55.8%\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"GRU multi-7L\", \"+5.5\", \"0\", \"41%\", \"78%±11%*\"]\n      - name: \"* Multi-seed mean±std\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 6: Lead time distribution histograms (3 models × 2 methods). All show right-skewed distributions centered near 0, with long positive tails (up to +30 steps) but substantial mass at negative values. Shape is consistent across models.\"\n      - \"Fig 6b: CDF of detection delay. Oracle CDF rises steeply from +20-40 steps, while all probe CDFs cross 50% near lead=0. The gap between oracle and best probe is consistent across all 3 models (~25-30 steps at 50th percentile).\"\n    analysis: \"Lead time unreliability replicates perfectly across architectures. The signature pattern—positive mean, zero/negative median—appears on all 3 models, establishing this as a fundamental property of reasoning model safety signals. The oracle gap (100% early detection vs ~40% for best probes) is consistent, indicating that hidden states encode current safety state accurately but poorly predict the future trajectory. GRU coverage instability also replicates (std 9-11% across seeds), confirming this reflects fundamental signal noise rather than model-specific limitations. This three-model consistency strengthens Claim 3 from 'observed on r1-8b' to 'universal property of reasoning model commitment dynamics.'\"\n\n  - name: \"TrajGuard Mahalanobis baseline (all 3 models)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: \"Mahalanobis distance baseline on 32B models. Expected to fail due to signal in minor PCs.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_md_best_crossing_rate: 0.279\n      r1_32b_md_best_config: \"pca_64_p75\"\n      r1_32b_md_best_lead: 14.8\n      r1_32b_safe_md_mean: 7.12\n      r1_32b_jb_pre_md_mean: 7.18\n      r1_32b_jb_post_md_mean: 7.08\n      r1_32b_delta_max: 0.019\n      qwq_32b_md_best_crossing_rate: 0.256\n      qwq_32b_md_best_config: \"pca_128_p75\"\n      qwq_32b_md_best_lead: 15.2\n      qwq_32b_safe_md_mean: 7.45\n      qwq_32b_jb_pre_md_mean: 7.51\n      qwq_32b_jb_post_md_mean: 7.39\n      qwq_32b_delta_max: 0.022\n      verdict: \"Fails on all 3 models. MD cannot distinguish safe from jailbreak traces.\"\n    result_rationale: \"The PCA analysis in exp-001 confirms Fisher collapse in 3D holds on 32B models (Fisher drops from ~18-19 to ~1.0 in PCA-3D). Since TrajGuard relies on Mahalanobis distance in the principal subspace, and the safety signal lives in minor components, the method fundamentally cannot separate safety states. The 32B results are slightly worse than r1-8b (crossing rate 26-28% vs 30%) because the 5120-d space distributes the signal across even more minor components. The near-zero delta between safe/jb-pre/jb-post MD values confirms that in the covariance-normalized space, safety commitment is invisible. This universally validates the 'existing detectors fail' claim.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: TrajGuard/Mahalanobis Failure Across Models\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Best Config\", \"Crossing Rate (k=5)\", \"MD(safe)\", \"MD(jb-pre)\", \"MD(jb-post)\", \"Max Δ\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"pca_64_p75\", \"30.2%\", \"6.66\", \"6.73\", \"6.64\", \"0.023\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"pca_64_p75\", \"27.9%\", \"7.12\", \"7.18\", \"7.08\", \"0.019\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"pca_128_p75\", \"25.6%\", \"7.45\", \"7.51\", \"7.39\", \"0.022\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 7: MD trajectory plots for safe vs jailbreak traces (3 models). Completely overlapping distributions in all cases. Annotated commitment points show no MD inflection.\"\n    analysis: \"Universal failure of geometric methods across all 3 models and architectures. The negligible delta between safe and jailbreak MD values (<0.025 across all models) confirms that in the variance-dominated subspace, safety commitment is undetectable. This is the empirical backing for our theoretical argument: safety signals in minor PCs require supervised probing, not unsupervised geometric methods. The consistency of failure across Llama-based and Qwen2.5-based models makes this a strong negative result about the entire class of TrajGuard-style detectors applied to reasoning model safety.\"\n\n  - name: \"Bootstrap confidence intervals + threshold-independent metrics\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: \"1000 bootstrap resamples on all key metrics across all 3 models. AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF, cross-validation variance.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_hs_temporal_coverage_ci: \"[0.442, 0.698]\"\n      r1_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr_ci: \"[0.082, 0.215]\"\n      r1_32b_hs_temporal_precision_ci: \"[0.498, 0.712]\"\n      qwq_32b_hs_temporal_coverage_ci: \"[0.419, 0.681]\"\n      qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr_ci: \"[0.091, 0.224]\"\n      qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precision_ci: \"[0.479, 0.695]\"\n      pooled_3model_hs_vs_text_precp_fpr_p: 0.003\n      pooled_3model_temporal_vs_static_coverage_p: 0.028\n      pooled_hs_precision_advantage_ci: \"[0.08, 0.18]\"\n      cross_model_effect_consistency_I2: 0.12\n      r1_32b_auroc_hs_temporal: 0.862\n      r1_32b_auroc_text_temporal: 0.781\n      qwq_32b_auroc_hs_temporal: 0.849\n      qwq_32b_auroc_text_temporal: 0.769\n    result_rationale: \"With n=43 test traces per model, individual CIs are wide (as observed in r1-8b exp-006 results). However, pooling across 3 models (n≈129 effective traces) narrows CIs substantially. The pooled HS-vs-text pre-CP FPR comparison achieves p=0.003 because the effect is large (ratio ≥1.7x) and consistent across all 3 models. The low I² statistic (0.12) indicates minimal heterogeneity—the effect size is remarkably consistent across architectures, which is the paper's strongest statistical argument. Individual per-model comparisons may not reach significance (p=0.05-0.15), but the meta-analytic combination across 3 models is decisive.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: Pooled Bootstrap Results (3 Models)\"\n        columns: [\"Comparison\", \"Effect Size\", \"95% CI\", \"p-value\", \"I²\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"HS vs Text pre-CP FPR\", \"-14.2pp\", \"[-8.1, -17.8]\", \"0.003\", \"0.12\"]\n          - [\"HS vs Text precision\", \"+12.4pp\", \"[+7.9, +16.1]\", \"0.001\", \"0.08\"]\n          - [\"Temporal vs Static coverage\", \"+5.8pp\", \"[+0.6, +10.4]\", \"0.028\", \"0.21\"]\n          - [\"GRU vs LR lead time\", \"+2.1 steps\", \"[-1.2, +5.8]\", \"0.14\", \"0.31\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 8: Forest plot of HS precision advantage across 3 models. Each model's point estimate with CI, plus pooled diamond. All point estimates positive (10-13pp), pooled CI excludes zero.\"\n      - \"Fig 8b: PR curves for HS temporal vs text temporal on each model. HS curve dominates text curve across all recall levels, consistently across all 3 models.\"\n      - \"Fig 8c: AUROC comparison bar chart (3 models × 2 modalities). HS temporal consistently 7-8pp above text temporal.\"\n    analysis: \"The three-model pooled analysis resolves the statistical power concern that plagued single-model results. While individual per-model p-values are marginal (0.05-0.15), the pooled comparison with I²=0.12 (minimal heterogeneity) achieves p=0.003 for the HS precision advantage. This is the correct statistical framing for the paper: consistent effect across multiple independent models, not single-model significance. The AUROC advantage (8-9pp) is threshold-independent confirmation that HS monitoring is decisively superior. Temporal vs static coverage reaches p=0.028 pooled, confirming Claim 2. GRU vs LR lead time doesn't reach significance (p=0.14), honestly acknowledging the noisy lead time signal.\"\n\n  - name: \"Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: \"Window size sweep on all 3 models to find optimal W and show diminishing returns.\"\n    expected_results:\n      optimal_w_r1_8b: 15\n      optimal_w_r1_32b: 15\n      optimal_w_qwq_32b: 20\n      coverage_at_w1_mean: 0.49\n      coverage_at_w5_mean: 0.52\n      coverage_at_w10_mean: 0.56\n      coverage_at_w15_mean: 0.60\n      coverage_at_w20_mean: 0.61\n      coverage_at_w25_mean: 0.60\n      diminishing_returns_elbow: \"W=10-15\"\n      precp_fpr_stable_across_w: true\n      precp_fpr_range_all_w: \"[0.13, 0.17]\"\n    result_rationale: \"Temporal context shows diminishing returns because the commitment point is a relatively local phenomenon (safety reasoning typically ceases within a 10-20 step window). W=15 captures the critical transition period; beyond that, earlier steps add noise without information. The optimal W is consistent across models (15-20) because reasoning model safety dynamics operate on similar timescales regardless of model size. Crucially, pre-CP FPR remains stable across all W values (13-17%), confirming that the HS precision advantage is not an artifact of window size selection—it's a property of the signal modality.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A1: Window Size Ablation\"\n        columns: [\"W\", \"r1-8b Cov\", \"r1-32b Cov\", \"QwQ Cov\", \"Mean Pre-CP FPR\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"1 (static)\", \"60.5%\", \"51.2%\", \"48.8%\", \"15.5%\"]\n          - [\"3\", \"61.2%\", \"52.4%\", \"49.5%\", \"15.2%\"]\n          - [\"5\", \"62.3%\", \"53.8%\", \"51.2%\", \"14.8%\"]\n          - [\"10\", \"63.8%\", \"56.1%\", \"54.0%\", \"14.5%\"]\n          - [\"15\", \"65.1%\", \"58.1%\", \"55.8%\", \"14.7%\"]\n          - [\"20\", \"64.8%\", \"58.4%\", \"56.9%\", \"15.1%\"]\n          - [\"25\", \"64.2%\", \"57.6%\", \"55.4%\", \"15.8%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A1: Coverage vs window size (3 models). Monotone increase until W=15-20, then plateau/slight decrease. Elbow at W=10-15.\"\n      - \"Fig A1b: Pre-CP FPR vs window size (3 models). Nearly flat lines across all W values, confirming FPR independence from temporal context width.\"\n    analysis: \"The diminishing returns curve peaks at W=15-20 across all models, with coverage gains saturating after the elbow. This is consistent with commitment dynamics: the critical transition from safety reasoning to harmful execution occurs within a ~15-step window, and additional context beyond that provides no incremental signal. The stability of pre-CP FPR across W values is important methodologically—it means the precision advantage of HS monitoring is robust to hyperparameter choices. For the paper, W=15 is the default recommendation, with acknowledgment that W=20 may be marginally better for models with longer traces.\"\n\n  - name: \"Layer selection ablation (all layers, 32B)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: \"Full layer sweep with temporal probe on 32B models. Confirm middle-layer finding generalizes.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_temporal_best_layer: 28\n      r1_32b_temporal_best_acc: 0.798\n      r1_32b_temporal_layer_range_within_2pp: \"[24, 34]\"\n      r1_32b_early_layers_1_10_mean_acc: 0.62\n      r1_32b_late_layers_55_64_mean_acc: 0.71\n      qwq_32b_temporal_best_layer: 30\n      qwq_32b_temporal_best_acc: 0.781\n      qwq_32b_temporal_layer_range_within_2pp: \"[26, 36]\"\n      middle_layer_advantage_over_last: \"+8-10pp\"\n      depth_correlation_with_r1_8b: 0.94\n    result_rationale: \"The middle-layer peak generalizes because of how transformer representations evolve: early layers encode syntactic/positional information, middle layers encode semantic/behavioral states (including safety reasoning state), and late layers specialize for next-token prediction. The safety commitment signal is a behavioral state property, hence middle-layer encoding. The peak being at ~44-47% depth (not 50%) is consistent across all models, suggesting the safety information is processed in the first half of the network and progressively refined. The broad plateau (±5 layers within 2pp of peak) means the exact layer choice is not critical—a practical advantage for deployment.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A2: Layer Selection (Temporal Probe)\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Best Layer\", \"Depth%\", \"Peak Acc\", \"Plateau Width (±2pp)\", \"Last Layer Acc\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b (32L)\", \"14\", \"44%\", \"0.848\", \"L10-L18\", \"0.761\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b (64L)\", \"28\", \"44%\", \"0.798\", \"L24-L34\", \"0.712\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B (64L)\", \"30\", \"47%\", \"0.781\", \"L26-L36\", \"0.698\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A2: Temporal probe accuracy vs layer (all layers, 3 models). X-axis normalized to depth percentage. All three curves peak at 44-47% depth with similar shape—inverted U with broad plateau. Includes shaded ±1 std band from CV folds.\"\n    analysis: \"The middle-layer finding is universal across architectures and scales. The depth-normalized accuracy curves are nearly identical (correlation 0.94 between r1-8b and 32B models), confirming that safety state encoding follows the same depth-dependent pattern regardless of total layer count. The broad plateau (10+ layers within 2pp of peak) is practically important: it means the method is robust to layer selection, and a simple heuristic (select layer at 44% depth) works across models without per-model tuning.\"\n\n  - name: \"16-label taxonomy supervision\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: \"Use fine-grained Type 1/2/3/4 labels instead of binary commitment for richer training signal.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_8b_16label_coverage: 0.674\n      r1_8b_16label_precp_fpr: 0.151\n      r1_8b_16label_precision: 0.641\n      r1_8b_16label_lead: 5.4\n      r1_8b_improvement_over_binary: \"+2.3pp coverage, +2pp precision\"\n      r1_32b_16label_coverage: 0.605\n      r1_32b_16label_precp_fpr: 0.138\n      r1_32b_16label_precision: 0.618\n      transition_type_most_informative: \"Type 3 (safety-to-harmful transition)\"\n      diminishing_returns_vs_binary: \"modest\"\n    result_rationale: \"Fine-grained labels provide modest improvement because the binary commitment signal already captures the primary discriminative information. The 16-label taxonomy helps most at the transition boundaries (Type 3: safety→harmful), where the additional supervision helps the probe distinguish 'approaching commitment' from 'already committed.' However, the improvement is modest (+2-3pp) because the temporal window already captures this transition implicitly. The main value is diagnostic: confirming which label types are most informative for detection timing. This is a P1 experiment because the improvement doesn't change the paper's conclusions.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A3: Binary vs 16-Label Supervision\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Supervision\", \"Coverage\", \"Pre-CP FPR\", \"Precision\", \"Lead\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"Binary W=15\", \"65.1%\", \"16.0%\", \"58.3%\", \"+4.9\"]\n          - [\"r1-8b\", \"16-label W=15\", \"67.4%\", \"15.1%\", \"64.1%\", \"+5.4\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"Binary W=15\", \"58.1%\", \"14.2%\", \"60.1%\", \"+5.8\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"16-label W=15\", \"60.5%\", \"13.8%\", \"61.8%\", \"+6.1\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A3: Per-label-type probe confidence trajectories showing which transitions are most detectable. Type 3 (safety→harmful) shows sharpest confidence increase, consistent with commitment point definition.\"\n    analysis: \"16-label supervision provides modest but consistent improvement (+2-3pp coverage and precision). The improvement is largest for traces where the binary label masks a gradual transition through multiple safety states before commitment. However, the gains are small enough that binary supervision remains the recommended default for simplicity. The per-type analysis confirms that Type 3 transitions are most informative, which aligns with the commitment point definition and provides theoretical validation.\"\n\n  - name: \"Oracle decomposition\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: \"Analyze what oracle labels encode that hidden states don't. Decompose the +31-step oracle advantage.\"\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_advantage_source_breakdown:\n        future_label_pattern: \"62%\"\n        multi_step_trajectory: \"28%\"\n        global_trace_context: \"10%\"\n      oracle_K1_early_rate: 0.837\n      probe_best_early_rate: 0.42\n      gap_attributable_to_future_info: \"72%\"\n      gap_attributable_to_training_signal: \"28%\"\n      key_insight: \"Oracle uses FUTURE labels; probes can only use PAST hidden states. The commitment point is fundamentally unpredictable from pre-commitment representations because the model has not yet 'decided.'\"\n    result_rationale: \"The oracle achieves 100% detection with +31 mean lead because it has access to future labels (it knows the commitment point by definition and can look ahead K steps). The probe can only access past/current hidden states, and the model's representations before commitment encode the CURRENT safety reasoning state, not the FUTURE trajectory. Decomposition shows that 62% of the oracle advantage comes from future label information (which is definitionally unavailable to any real-time method), 28% from multi-step trajectory shape (partially capturable by GRU but noisy), and 10% from global trace context. This establishes the gap as principled: no method operating in real-time can close >72% of the oracle gap.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 9: Oracle Advantage Decomposition\"\n        columns: [\"Information Source\", \"% of Oracle Advantage\", \"Accessible to Probes?\", \"Method Exploiting It\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Future labels (K-step lookahead)\", \"62%\", \"No (definitional)\", \"None possible\"]\n          - [\"Multi-step trajectory shape\", \"28%\", \"Partially (noisy)\", \"GRU/temporal\"]\n          - [\"Global trace context (trace length, topic)\", \"10%\", \"Yes\", \"Features, but low signal\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 9: Ablation showing oracle performance as information sources are progressively removed. Removing future labels drops oracle from 100% to ~38% early detection (matching probe performance). This gap is irreducible.\"\n    analysis: \"The oracle decomposition establishes that pre-textual detection is fundamentally limited by information asymmetry: the commitment point is a future event whose timing cannot be reliably predicted from current representations. The model's hidden states before commitment encode ongoing safety reasoning but do NOT encode when that reasoning will terminate. This is the paper's key theoretical insight for Claim 3: unreliable pre-textual detection is not a method limitation but an information-theoretic constraint. Future work would need to identify pre-commitment signals that are predictive of commitment timing, which our analysis suggests are either absent or extremely subtle.\"\n\n  - name: \"OpenThinker-7B (4th model, scale control)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: \"Qwen2.5-7B-based reasoning model. 2×2 matrix: Llama×Qwen2.5 × 7-8B×32B.\"\n    expected_results:\n      openthinker_best_layer: 14\n      openthinker_best_layer_depth_pct: 0.50\n      openthinker_static_acc: 0.795\n      openthinker_static_precp_fpr: 0.172\n      openthinker_static_precision: 0.589\n      openthinker_temporal_coverage: 0.628\n      openthinker_temporal_coverage_delta: +0.058\n      openthinker_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.165\n      openthinker_temporal_precision: 0.571\n      openthinker_gru_lead_mean: 4.1\n      openthinker_gru_lead_median: 0\n      scale_effect_7b_vs_32b_precision_delta: \"-2.5pp\"\n      architecture_effect_llama_vs_qwen_precision_delta: \"-1.8pp\"\n    result_rationale: \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base) fills the 2×2 design matrix. Its results should be intermediate: same architecture family as QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5) but same scale as r1-8b (7-8B parameters). If scale dominates architecture, OpenThinker should match r1-8b; if architecture dominates, it should match QwQ-32B. In the best tier, both factors contribute modestly but neither dominates—the signal quality is primarily determined by the reasoning training procedure, not base model properties. Pre-CP FPR at 0.172 is within the [0.12, 0.20] range, confirming the HS advantage holds at all tested scales and architectures.\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A4: 2×2 Architecture×Scale Matrix\"\n        columns: [\"Architecture\", \"Scale\", \"Model\", \"Pre-CP FPR\", \"Precision\", \"Temporal Δ\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Llama\", \"8B\", \"r1-8b\", \"16.0%\", \"58.3%\", \"+4.6pp\"]\n          - [\"Qwen2.5\", \"7B\", \"OpenThinker-7B\", \"16.5%\", \"57.1%\", \"+5.8pp\"]\n          - [\"Qwen2.5\", \"32B\", \"r1-32b\", \"14.2%\", \"60.1%\", \"+6.9pp\"]\n          - [\"Qwen2.5\", \"32B\", \"QwQ-32B\", \"14.9%\", \"58.4%\", \"+7.0pp\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A4: 2×2 interaction plot. X-axis: scale (7-8B vs 32B). Lines: architecture (Llama, Qwen2.5). Near-parallel lines indicate additive effects without strong interaction. Both factors contribute ~2pp to precision.\"\n    analysis: \"The 2×2 matrix reveals that both architecture and scale contribute modestly to probe quality, but neither is dominant—the primary determinant is the presence of reasoning training. The near-parallel lines in the interaction plot indicate no architecture×scale interaction, meaning the findings generalize cleanly. This result strengthens the paper's universality claim: safety commitment detection works consistently across the tested space of reasoning models. Scale provides a modest 2-3pp precision advantage (likely due to richer representations), and architecture contributes similarly.\"\n\n  - name: \"GRU/Attention probe on 32B models\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: \"GRU h=256 multi-layer on 32B models. Verify instability pattern and 'simplicity is optimal' conclusion.\"\n    expected_results:\n      r1_32b_gru_coverage_mean: 0.81\n      r1_32b_gru_coverage_std: 0.09\n      r1_32b_gru_coverage_range: \"[0.72, 0.91]\"\n      r1_32b_gru_precp_fpr: 0.195\n      r1_32b_gru_precision: 0.612\n      r1_32b_gru_safe_fpr: 0.58\n      qwq_32b_gru_coverage_mean: 0.78\n      qwq_32b_gru_coverage_std: 0.11\n      qwq_32b_gru_coverage_range: \"[0.67, 0.89]\"\n      qwq_32b_gru_precp_fpr: 0.208\n      qwq_32b_gru_precision: 0.591\n      qwq_32b_gru_safe_fpr: 0.61\n      gru_vs_lr_precision_advantage: \"negligible (+1-3pp)\"\n      gru_vs_lr_safe_fpr_penalty: \"+20pp\"\n      verdict: \"GRU trades precision for coverage; LR remains preferred for pre-commitment monitoring.\"\n    result_rationale: \"GRU instability replicates on 32B models because it's driven by training data scarcity (n=125 training traces) and the fundamentally noisy commitment signal, not model-specific properties. The std of 9-11% across seeds confirms that GRU's capacity exceeds what the training data can stably supervise. On 32B, GRU achieves marginally higher coverage (78-81%) but at the cost of substantially higher safe FPR (58-61% vs LR's 35-37%). This confirms the 'simplicity is optimal' narrative: for the critical pre-commitment detection task, temporal LR provides the best precision/FPR tradeoff, while GRU is useful only if coverage is prioritized over precision. The paper frames this as: 'more complex models can detect more commitment points but at unacceptable false positive cost.'\"\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table A5: GRU vs LR on 32B Models (5-seed mean±std)\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Method\", \"Coverage\", \"Pre-CP FPR\", \"Precision\", \"Safe FPR\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"Temporal LR W=15\", \"58.1%±3.2%\", \"14.2%\", \"60.1%\", \"35.5%\"]\n          - [\"r1-32b\", \"GRU multi-7L\", \"81%±9%\", \"19.5%\", \"61.2%\", \"58%\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"Temporal LR W=15\", \"55.8%±3.8%\", \"14.9%\", \"58.4%\", \"36.8%\"]\n          - [\"QwQ-32B\", \"GRU multi-7L\", \"78%±11%\", \"20.8%\", \"59.1%\", \"61%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig A5: Coverage vs safe FPR tradeoff plot (Pareto front). LR methods cluster in the low-FPR/moderate-coverage region; GRU methods in the high-coverage/high-FPR region. No method achieves both high coverage AND low FPR.\"\n      - \"Fig A5b: GRU coverage distribution across 5 seeds (violin plots, 3 models). Wide distributions confirm instability is universal.\"\n    analysis: \"GRU instability replicating on 32B models confirms it's a fundamental signal property: with n=125 training traces, the GRU's capacity leads to overfitting to spurious temporal patterns that vary across initializations. The coverage-vs-FPR tradeoff is universal: GRU gains ~20pp coverage over LR but pays ~22pp in safe FPR. For deployment scenarios prioritizing precision in the pre-commitment window (the paper's primary framing), temporal LR remains optimal. This strengthens the 'simplicity is optimal for this task' conclusion—not because complex models can't learn, but because the training data is insufficient to stably train them.\"\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"92317e92-59ba-40d8-b9f8-d311cd7b1e86","total_cost_usd":0.433151,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13002,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":13516,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13002,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":13516,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13002,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13002},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":7963,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.008058,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":13516,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":13002,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.425093,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"7a906be5-1b7c-46e1-84f6-9f6a80746403"}
